#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77015ca5-d392-500e-93dc-8e065dd1c06c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "7fcfa422-4cc0-5798-8bc3-4824de1a3826" :question/math-academy-id "e-13266" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(Z < -1.54)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, -1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.063 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0618 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0606 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0749 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0735 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0918 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0901 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0885 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, then by the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$,\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z)\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Z$ is continuous,\n\n$$\nP(Z < -1.54) = P(Z \\leq -1.54) = \\Phi(-1.54)\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/265/Source/Images/4d608e3553d1fa62d9b86ec8acae1182.png)\n\nNow, we determine the value of $\\Phi(-1.54)$ from the table:\n\n- We first select the row that corresponds to $-1.5$ and the column that corresponds to $0.04$.\n- Then, we take the value that lies at the intersection:\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, -1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.063 \\,$ | $\\color{blue}\\, 0.0618 \\,$ | $\\,0.0606\\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0749 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0735 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0918 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0901 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0885 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, according to the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq -1.54) = \\Phi(-1.54) = 0.0618\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e376bffa-02ae-5575-af2c-4ad5e703f9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "a376a597-d6ab-5d51-8c12-196ed793150d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13267" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(Z \\geq -1.16)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, -1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0901 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0885 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0869 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1075 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1056 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1038 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1271 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1251 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1230 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute\n\n$$\nP(Z \\geq -1.16)\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/265/Source/Images/7d89d5410f6ee87e310a09801bd13918.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$,\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z)\n$$\n\nSince the total area under the graph equals $1$, we can express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z \\ge - 1.16) &= 1 - P(Z < - 1.16) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(-1.16)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/265/Source/Images/53dbf3c2f639c2ac352520f9f98d5008.png)\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-1.16) = 0.1230\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z \\ge - 1.16) &= 1 - Φ(-1.16) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.1230 \\\\\n&= 0.8770\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059351 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "607e0800-870c-5271-9220-0489d9c5b891" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "d95c5015-9dc4-50a5-85bc-50bab9bf0650" :question/math-academy-id "e-13268" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(-0.48 < Z < 1.18)$.\n\n| $z$$\\, 0.07 \\,$$\\, 0.08 \\,$$\\, 0.09 \\,$$\\, -0.5 \\,$ $\\, 0.2843 \\,$ $\\, 0.2810 \\,$ $\\, 0.2776 \\,$ $\\, -0.4 \\,$ $\\, 0.3192 \\,$ $\\, 0.3156 \\,$ $\\, 0.3121 \\,$ $\\, -0.3 \\,$ $\\, 0.3557 \\,$ $\\, 0.3520 \\,$ $\\, 0.3483 \\,$ |  | $z$$\\, 0.07 \\,$$\\, 0.08 \\,$$\\, 0.09 \\,$$\\, 1.0 \\,$ $\\, 0.8577 \\,$ $\\, 0.8599 \\,$ $\\, 0.8621 \\,$ $\\, 1.1 \\,$ $\\, 0.8790 \\,$ $\\, 0.8810 \\,$ $\\, 0.8830 \\,$ $\\, 1.2 \\,$ $\\, 0.8980 \\,$ $\\, 0.8997 \\,$ $\\, 0.9015 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(-0.48 < Z < 1.18)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/265/Source/Images/871c67fcea91249ce4ac5dd6d527df33.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$,\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z)\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.48 < Z < 1.18) &= P(Z < 1.18) - P(Z \\le - 0.48) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 1.18) - P(Z \\le - 0.48) \\\\\n&= Φ(1.18) - Φ(-0.48)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ(-0.48) &= 0.3156 \\\\\nΦ(1.18) &= 0.8810\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.48 < Z < 1.18) &= Φ(1.18) - Φ(-0.48) \\\\\n&= 0.8810 - 0.3156 \\\\\n&= 0.5654\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "607e0800-870c-5271-9220-0489d9c5b891" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c678d4fd-83cd-5a52-902d-f8326f03b50c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0529" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059198} {:answer/id #uuid "25d9136a-3161-5664-ae6a-0c740c27d138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0987" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059199} {:answer/id #uuid "52cb0a29-8496-5646-b2a9-b0fae26959fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0678" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059200} {:answer/id #uuid "686a29b4-a276-5a84-9d45-572cada7cfcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059201} {:answer/id #uuid "6db45a8c-ae03-5810-8fad-d407afc80f3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0543" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "686a29b4-a276-5a84-9d45-572cada7cfcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059201} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cdcc936-2d7f-5637-ba1c-776c3c6619db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059197}] :question/id #uuid "cd599733-7c2f-538f-a1fa-ca130a6c4dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94195" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0, 1)$, compute $P(0.70 < Z < 0.92)$.\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.7$ | $0.7580$ | $0.7611$ | $0.7642$ |\n| $0.8$ | $0.7881$ | $0.7910$ | $0.7939$ |\n| $0.9$ | $0.8159$ | $0.8186$ | $0.8212$ |" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
